## Context

Surfaced by the PR #1042 code review (ticket 0566): `_spearman()` in
`src/aedist/screen_validation_within_model.py` assigns ordinal ranks
with no tie correction — tied values get their natural-sort position
instead of the average of tied ranks (the standard Spearman estimator).
On the 70-run pooled data this yields ρ = 0.9166 vs scipy's 0.9195;
both round to the published 0.92, so the manuscript claim is unaffected,
but the committed 3dp artifact value (0.917) would not match an
independent scipy replication (0.920). Pre-existing limitation, not a
regression; the same ranks feed the per-model positive-direction counts.

Adjacent hardening (same review, optional): `tests/test_manuscript_macros.py`
`float(_screen_metric("pooled_spearman_cap_distinct"))` raises ValueError
on an empty-value CSV row; guard or skip for the None case.

## Actions

1. Switch `_spearman()` to average-rank (midrank) tie handling.
2. Regenerate the screen-validation artifacts and macros; check whether
   the pooled value (expect 0.92 unchanged at 2dp) or any per-model
   count shifts; commit regenerated artifacts together.
3. If any published 2dp value shifts, STOP and escalate to the author
   before touching prose.
4. Add a unit test with deliberate ties pinning the midrank behaviour.

## Exit criteria

- [x] `_spearman()` matches scipy.stats.spearmanr on tied data (unit test)
- [x] Regenerated artifacts committed; published 2dp values verified
  unchanged or escalated
- [x] `make check` passes
